<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">East Asia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> is the eastern region of Asia, usually taken to include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. For two thousand years its societies shared Chinese characters, Confucian ideas of government and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a>, and were linked by a tribute system centred on the Chinese court. Today it is one of the most populous and economically important regions of the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-asia.jpg" alt="East Asia" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Koyos + Ssolbergj + Serg!o · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_Asia_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jin dynasty changed the political geography of <b>East Asia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Ivory and gold were carried down the Limpopo valley to coastal ports, first Chibuene and later Sofala, and in return came great quantities of glass beads from India and South-<b>East Asia</b>, as well as Chinese celadon, which has been found at the royal residence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mapungubwe">Kingdom of Mapungubwe · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ming Empire exerted profound influence within <b>East Asia</b> and beyond during the early modern period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ryukyu Kingdom turned a small chain of islands into one of <b>East Asia</b>'s leading maritime trading states, and its unusual double subordination to Ming and Qing China and to Japan's Satsuma Domain made it a meeting point of the Chinese and Japanese worlds until Meiji Japan annexed…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Divine Bell of King Seongdeok, completed in 771, is one of the largest surviving bells in Korea, and the Cheomseongdae tower at Gyeongju, traditionally dated to Queen Seondeok's reign, is often described as the oldest surviving observatory in <b>East Asia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A founding member of ASEAN in 1967, Singapore hosts the secretariats of APEC and the Pacific Economic Cooperation Council, belongs to the Commonwealth, the Non-Aligned Movement and the <b>East Asia</b> Summit and keeps diplomatic relations with more than 180 states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/singapore">Singapore · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
